     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is double the labeled maximum daily dose, which is 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin and 2,000 mg/day for extended-release in the US. Going over that limit raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity, and normal kidney and liver function doesn't change the limit.\n\nWas this a typo? Here are some options that stay within the label:\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day, the IR maximum.\n- **1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **500 mg PO twice daily with meals**, a typical starting dose if the patient is new to metformin. It is then titrated up.\n\nTell me which dose you want, or give me a different one, and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
